CAT = "cmaaComm"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("Which question to a patient at check-in is closed-ended?",
 "\"Did you bring your insurance card?\"", ["\"How can I help you today?\"", "\"What brings you in to see us today?\"", "\"Tell me about your concerns.\""],
 "A closed-ended question can be answered yes or no or with one fact. The other questions invite the patient to explain in her own words."),
("When is a closed-ended question the better choice?",
 "To confirm a phone number", ["To learn why a patient is upset", "To explore a patient's worries", "To open a new patient interview"],
 "Closed questions get a fast, exact answer, which suits confirming facts. Open questions suit feelings and concerns."),
("A caller says her son \"is sick again.\" Which follow-up is a probing question?",
 "\"What symptoms is he having now?\"", ["\"I'm sorry to hear that.\"", "\"Can you hold, please?\"", "\"Has he been seen in this office before?\""],
 "A probing question digs for detail about what the caller just said. Asking whether he has been seen before is a closed screening question, not a probe of the symptoms."),
("A caller says, \"You people never get anything right, and I want it fixed now.\" Which communication style is this?",
 "Aggressive", ["Assertive", "Passive", "Passive-aggressive"],
 "Aggressive communication blames and demands. Assertive communication states needs clearly while respecting the other person."),
("A medical assistant calmly says, \"I can't book that today, but I can offer Thursday at 9.\" Which communication style is this?",
 "Assertive", ["Aggressive", "Passive", "Submissive"],
 "Assertive communication is honest and direct about limits while offering a respectful option. A passive reply would give in or avoid the issue."),
("A patient's insurance card shows a legal first name she no longer uses. How should the front desk address her?",
 "By the name she asks to be called", ["By the name printed on her card", "By her last name only", "By whichever name the chart lists"],
 "Staff use the name the patient asks for in conversation. The legal name stays in registration and on claims so they match the insurer's records."),
("A patient tells the front desk that their pronouns are they and them. What should the medical assistant do?",
 "Record and use those pronouns", ["Use pronouns that match the ID", "Avoid using any pronouns at all", "Ask the provider which to use"],
 "Respectful care uses the pronouns a patient gives, and many EHRs have a field to record them so every staff member sees them."),
("A provider tells a patient he has hypertension. How should the medical assistant put this in plain words?",
 "High blood pressure", ["Low blood pressure", "A fast, pounding heartbeat", "High blood sugar"],
 "Hyper- means above and tension refers to pressure in the arteries. High blood sugar is hyperglycemia."),
("A patient asks the front desk which of two orthopedic surgeons she should choose. Which reply stays within scope?",
 "Give details on both and let her decide", ["Recommend the one you like best", "Tell her she will need surgery", "Say the closer surgeon is the better one"],
 "Front office staff can share factual details such as location, hours and whether each is in network. Judging skill or predicting treatment is outside their role."),
("Which behavior shows active listening while a patient explains a billing problem?",
 "Restating the problem to confirm it", ["Planning a reply while she talks", "Typing other notes meanwhile", "Finishing each of her sentences for her"],
 "Active listening means full attention and checking understanding, for example by restating what was heard. Multitasking and interrupting signal that the listener is not engaged."),
("A patient says she is worn out caring for her mother at home. Which reply shows empathy?",
 "\"That sounds like a lot to manage.\"", ["\"I know exactly how you feel.\"", "\"At least she is still here with you.\"", "\"Everyone goes through that.\""],
 "Empathy names and accepts the other person's feelings. Claiming to know exactly how she feels, or minimizing it, shuts the conversation down."),
("In the communication cycle, what is the receiver's job?",
 "To decode the message", ["To encode the message", "To pick the channel", "To create the message"],
 "The sender creates and encodes the message and chooses a channel; the receiver decodes it and then gives feedback."),
("A patient misses check-in instructions because a TV is blaring in the waiting room. In the communication cycle, what is the TV?",
 "Noise", ["Feedback", "Channel", "Encoding"],
 "Noise is anything that interferes with the message reaching the receiver, such as sound, distraction or discomfort."),
("A patient demands a refund of a copay, and the front desk has no authority to give one. What is the best next step?",
 "Refer her to the billing lead", ["Refund it from petty cash", "Refuse and end the conversation", "Promise her a refund next week"],
 "Following the chain of command sends the request to someone who can decide. Promising or refunding without authority creates a bigger problem."),
("A caller is mildly annoyed that the provider is running 10 minutes late. What should the medical assistant do?",
 "Apologize and give a wait estimate", ["Transfer the call to the office manager", "File a formal complaint report", "Ask the provider to call back"],
 "A small complaint the front desk can resolve does not need escalation. An apology and honest information usually settle it."),
("A father whose name is on the child's chart asks for his son's records, and a court order is on file. What should the medical assistant check first?",
 "What the court order on file allows", ["Whether he has the child's insurance card", "Whether the mother is in the room", "Whether he paid the last bill"],
 "A parent is usually the child's personal representative, but a custody order can limit that. Staff check the order before releasing anything."),
("A patient is upset that her referral has not been sent. Which phrase helps de-escalate?",
 "\"Let's look at it together now.\"", ["\"That's not my department.\"", "\"Calm down, please.\"", "\"You should have called sooner.\""],
 "Offering to act right away shows the concern is taken seriously. Telling an upset person to calm down or blaming her usually makes things worse."),
("Two front desk staff both want the same week off. Which conflict style looks for an answer that fully meets both needs?",
 "Collaborating", ["Compromising", "Avoiding", "Accommodating"],
 "Collaborating works toward a solution that satisfies both sides. Compromising has each side give something up."),
("Which practice fits professional email to a patient?",
 "A clear subject and a brief message", ["All capital letters to show it is urgent", "Emoji to show warmth", "Text-message abbreviations"],
 "Professional email is concise, clearly labeled and free of slang. All capitals read as shouting."),
("Before emailing PHI to an outside office, what should the medical assistant confirm?",
 "That the method is secure and approved", ["That the diagnosis is in the subject line", "That all front desk staff are copied", "That it is sent after office hours"],
 "PHI is sent only by a method the practice has approved, such as encrypted email or a secure portal, and only to the right recipient."),
("A patient calls about a new rash, and the provider needs to know. In an office that uses an EHR, what is the best way to pass this on?",
 "An EHR message in her chart", ["A sticky note on the monitor", "A text from a personal phone", "A note left at the front desk"],
 "An EHR message reaches the right person, is tied to the patient and becomes part of the record. Notes and personal texts can be lost or seen by others."),
("Why should staff send patient questions through the EHR's message template rather than a general chat app?",
 "It is saved in her record", ["It reaches the patient faster", "It skips the provider's review", "It needs no login to use"],
 "EHR messaging keeps the communication in the chart and inside the practice's security controls."),
("A patient calls to cancel and mentions she has stopped taking her blood pressure pill. What should happen besides the cancellation?",
 "Note it and route it to the provider", ["Cancel only, since she called to cancel", "Tell her it is fine to stop", "Wait until her next visit to note it"],
 "Stopping a medicine is clinical information, so it is documented and sent to the provider. Front office staff never advise on medication."),
("A letter about a missed referral is mailed to a patient. What should go in her record?",
 "A copy of the letter", ["Nothing, since it was mailed", "Only the postage cost", "The mail carrier's name"],
 "Correspondence about a patient's care is part of the record, so a copy or note of what was sent and when is kept."),
("A patient says she cannot afford food since losing her job. Which resource fits best?",
 "A local food assistance program", ["A referral to a medical specialist", "A payment plan through collections", "A login for the patient portal"],
 "Front office staff often share community resources such as food banks and assistance programs when a patient mentions a social need."),
("A recently widowed patient says he feels alone. Which community resource is most fitting to share?",
 "A grief support group", ["A home health agency", "A medical equipment supplier", "A workers' compensation office"],
 "A grief support group addresses loss and isolation. Home health and equipment suppliers meet physical care needs."),
("A provider orders a fasting blood test for tomorrow morning. What should the written instructions tell the patient?",
 "Not to eat for the hours ordered", ["To eat a large meal first", "To drink no water at all", "To stop all of her usual medicines"],
 "Fasting instructions give the hours without food that the provider ordered. Water is usually allowed, and medicine changes come only from the provider."),
("Why should verbal pre-test instructions also be given in writing?",
 "So she can recheck them at home", ["So the office can bill for them", "Because verbal ones are not allowed", "So the patient need not listen"],
 "People forget much of what they hear, so written instructions back up the conversation and reduce missed preparations."),
("Which behavior at check-in may signal low health literacy?",
 "Asking to fill in forms at home", ["Arriving 15 minutes early", "Bringing a full medication list", "Asking where to park"],
 "Patients who struggle to read may avoid forms with excuses such as forgetting glasses or wanting to finish at home. Staff can offer help privately."),
("A patient with low vision must complete intake forms. What should the medical assistant offer?",
 "To read the forms aloud privately", ["To let another waiting patient help her", "To skip the forms for today", "To mail them to her next week"],
 "Reading forms in a private spot keeps her information confidential and lets her complete them that day."),
("An 85-year-old patient speaks slowly and takes time to answer questions. What should the medical assistant do?",
 "Allow extra time without rushing", ["Speak louder from the start", "Ask his daughter to answer instead", "Finish his answers for him"],
 "Older adults may need more time to respond. Raising the voice assumes hearing loss, and talking past him to family ignores him."),
("Which choice keeps a professional boundary with patients?",
 "Declining a patient's friend request", ["Sharing a cell number with a patient", "Lending a patient money", "Dating a current patient"],
 "Professional boundaries keep the relationship about care. Personal social media links, loans and dating all cross them."),
("Which choice fits professional appearance at a medical front desk?",
 "Neat clothes and an ID badge", ["A strong perfume or a cologne", "Flip-flops and shorts", "A headset playing music"],
 "A neat appearance and a visible name badge build trust. Strong scents can bother patients with allergies or asthma."),
("Which action best supports teamwork at the front desk?",
 "Helping when a coworker falls behind", ["Keeping duties strictly separate", "Taking only your own calls", "Leaving unfinished tasks for the next shift"],
 "Teamwork means sharing the load and helping others reach the team's goals, such as keeping the waiting room moving."),
("A lab calls with a critical result for a patient, and the medical assistant writes it down. What should she do next?",
 "Read it back to confirm it", ["Hang up and file it", "Wait for the fax copy", "Tell the patient the result first"],
 "Reading back a verbal report catches errors before the result goes to the provider, a step The Joint Commission's patient safety goals stress."),
("When calling an insurer about a claim, what should the medical assistant record?",
 "The rep's name, date and reference number", ["Only the claim's dollar total", "The rep's view of the provider", "Nothing, as long as the issue got resolved"],
 "Documenting who was spoken to, when, and the call reference number supports any later follow-up or appeal."),
]
